course: dictionary
lesson: 2876
title: One word, many shapes
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. Donut is a good example. Most of the time it is a small, sweet, fried cake, often shaped like a ring with a hole in the middle, or a round one filled with jam or cream.
explain: Context tells you which meaning is right. In a maths problem, a box of donuts is food, and a dozen donuts means 12. In geometry, a ring-shaped solid is called a donut shape, or a torus.
explain: Now read this: «It were me, you donut!» Donut is not food here. Everyone is laughing, so it is a joke: the speaker is calling a friend a silly name. This is slang, not one of the meanings above, so the clues in the sentence are what show you the meaning.
fact: Donut is a noun: a donut, two donuts.
fact: «Donut» means a small, sweet, fried cake, often ring-shaped or filled with jam or cream.
fact: In maths, a donut shape is called a torus.
fact: «Doing donuts» is informal, mainly US, and means driving a car round and round in tight circles.
fact: To choose the right meaning, read the words around the word and ask which meaning fits.
exercise: «Mum bought a box of donuts for the party.» What is a donut here? | *A small, sweet, fried cake | A ring-shaped shape in maths | A kind of toy | A car going round in circles
exercise: In a maths problem, a dozen donuts is how many donuts? | *12 | 6 | 10 | 24
exercise: In geometry, what is a donut shape called? | A pyramid | A cube | A circle | *A torus
test: «Dad shouted, "Stop doing donuts in the car park!" What does doing donuts mean? | *Driving round and round in tight circles | Baking in the kitchen | Rolling down a hill | Eating lots of cakes
test: «It were me, you donut!» Is donut a cake in this sentence? | No, it is a kind of car | *No, it is a teasing name for a silly person | Yes, a ring-shaped cake with jam | Yes, a sweet fried cake
exam: Which meaning of donut fits «a jam donut» on a plate? | A car doing donuts | A ring of keys | *A small, sweet, fried cake filled with jam | A torus in a maths book
